    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d702">

                <pb facs="1283" n="1159" sameAs="frus:frus1881/pg1159"/>

                <head>No. 698.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Blaine to Mr. Fish.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1881-05-26"><hi rendition="#italic">May</hi> 26,
                        1881</date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 213.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have to acknowledge the receipt of your
                    No. 397, and to express my satisfaction respecting your course in the case of
                    Eberhard, <pb facs="1284" n="1160" xml:id="pg1160"/> whose emigration to the
                    United States would evidently have proved undesirable.</p>
                <p>The communication of the facts mentioned in your No. 400, in the case of the
                    Oliver family, to the collector of customs at New York is approved.</p>
                <p>A copy of your No. 403 will be sent to the State board of charities, New York, as
                    you suggest.</p>
                <p>I am, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>JAMES G. BLAINE.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
